<speak>
<p>The delta theorem is the independent arithmetic branch of our proof. We allow Gaussian integer coefficients because Node three's polynomial g already has them. The result applies to any polynomial with the required norm identity, beyond the Hadamard construction.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Begin with nine cyclic positions and a Gaussian integer polynomial f whose product with its star is nine. Substitute any ninth root of unity. Star becomes complex conjugation, so the squared absolute value of the output is nine. Every output lies on a circle of radius three. These output values are different from the input roots.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For a concrete example, take f of P equal to three i P. Its star is negative three i P inverse, and their product is nine. Each input rotates ninety degrees and scales by three. The nine outputs are equally spaced for this particular example. It is a Gaussian group ring polynomial, not a Hadamard sign row.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Compare the outputs at one and omega, a primitive cube root of unity. Define delta of f as f of one divided by f of omega. Their magnitudes cancel. The quotient records the numerator's phase minus the denominator's phase: the rotation from f of omega to f of one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For our example, those angles are ninety and two hundred ten degrees. Their difference is negative one hundred twenty degrees. Thus delta is one over omega, or omega squared. Turn clockwise from the denominator to the numerator. Copy that same rotation onto the positive real direction of a separate unit circle. The quotient sits there, not at either original output point.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For general odd u greater than one, let I be its distinct prime divisors. Choose a primitive pth root for each prime p. For a subset S, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S; the empty subset gives one. Every such input raised to u squared equals one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Assume f has Gaussian integer coefficients and product with its star equal to u squared. Every evaluation is nonzero. Define delta by multiplying evaluations from even sized subsets and dividing by those from odd sized subsets. The empty subset supplies f of one in the numerator.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>With u equal to three, this is our original quotient. With two distinct primes p and q, the numerator is f of one times f at the product of their roots. The denominator is f at the pth root times f at the qth root. This is a ratio of ratios: phases add and subtract. Repeated powers of one prime create no new subset direction.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Why does this product have finite order? Kronecker's theorem says that an algebraic integer whose algebraic conjugates all have absolute value one is a root of unity. The norm identity gives every conjugate of delta absolute value one: equal counts of numerator and denominator factors cancel their magnitudes. An arithmetic comparison of the evaluations supplies algebraic integrality. Together, these two inputs allow Kronecker's theorem.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>A separate congruence argument at the odd primes shows that this root of unity has odd order. Kronecker alone does not supply that extra conclusion. For the nine position case, the full argument leaves just one, omega, or omega squared as the possible values of delta.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Return to c, d, and g. Each has product with its star equal to u squared, so the theorem makes all three delta values roots of unity of odd order. Carry this arithmetic input to Node six. The other branch brings congruences inherited from their common signs. Those are different ingredients, which we will combine next.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
